NNOOTTEESS
       The ggeetteennttrrooppyy() function is implemented using ggeettrraannddoomm(2).

       Whereas the glibc  wrapper  makes  ggeettrraannddoomm(2)  a  cancelation  point,
       ggeetteennttrrooppyy() is not a cancelation point.

       ggeetteennttrrooppyy()  is  also  declared  in  <<ssyyss//rraannddoomm..hh>>.  (No feature test
       macro need be defined to obtain the declaration from that header file.)

       A call to ggeetteennttrrooppyy() may block if the system has just booted and  the
       kernel  has  not  yet collected enough randomness to initialize the en‐
       tropy pool.  In this case, ggeetteennttrrooppyy() will keep blocking  even  if  a
       signal  is handled, and will return only once the entropy pool has been
       initialized.

